1
00:00:00,493 --> 00:00:02,480
Imagine you're importing cocoa from another

2
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:05,061
country. Before you can confidently accept a

3
00:00:05,081 --> 00:00:07,415
shipment, you need to understand where that

4
00:00:07,435 --> 00:00:09,742
cocoa came from and whether the supporting

5
00:00:09,762 --> 00:00:11,423
land evidence is consistent.

6
00:00:11,756 --> 00:00:13,811
But that information can be spread across

7
00:00:13,830 --> 00:00:16,682
supplier spreadsheets, shipment documents, and

8
00:00:16,702 --> 00:00:19,568
satellite data. A shipment might look complete

9
00:00:19,588 --> 00:00:22,254
on paper while important details are missing

10
00:00:22,274 --> 00:00:25,153
or contradictory. That's the problem LotHold

11
00:00:25,173 --> 00:00:26,734
is built to help solve.

12
00:00:28,082 --> 00:00:31,053
LotHold brings those records together into one

13
00:00:31,073 --> 00:00:34,338
review workflow. It helps cocoa exporters and

14
00:00:34,359 --> 00:00:37,503
importers organize shipment evidence, identify

15
00:00:37,524 --> 00:00:40,641
inconsistencies, examine available satellite

16
00:00:40,661 --> 00:00:43,442
observations, and document their decisions.

17
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:45,400
Let me show you how it works.

18
00:00:46,821 --> 00:00:49,460
First, I create a case for a shipment and

19
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:51,170
upload the supplier records.

20
00:00:51,487 --> 00:00:54,021
LotHold reads the information and shows me

21
00:00:54,042 --> 00:00:55,438
exactly what it found.

22
00:00:55,757 --> 00:00:58,630
I can inspect the quantities, land references,

23
00:00:58,709 --> 00:01:00,716
and source files before accepting them.

24
00:01:01,050 --> 00:01:03,317
This matters because uploading a document

25
00:01:03,337 --> 00:01:05,644
doesn't automatically mean its information is

26
00:01:05,664 --> 00:01:08,606
correct. The reviewer stays in control.

27
00:01:09,962 --> 00:01:12,908
Next, LotHold examines published satellite

28
00:01:12,928 --> 00:01:14,803
data for the supplied locations.

29
00:01:15,162 --> 00:01:17,855
Here, we can see observations about tree-cover

30
00:01:17,875 --> 00:01:20,355
loss, including the years associated with

31
00:01:20,375 --> 00:01:21,558
those observations.

32
00:01:21,891 --> 00:01:24,317
These results help identify areas that may

33
00:01:24,337 --> 00:01:26,093
need closer investigation.

34
00:01:26,425 --> 00:01:28,998
But satellite observations alone don't prove

35
00:01:29,018 --> 00:01:31,758
that a supplier broke a law or that a shipment

36
00:01:31,851 --> 00:01:34,623
is non-compliant. They're evidence for a human

37
00:01:34,643 --> 00:01:36,203
reviewer to consider.

38
00:01:37,562 --> 00:01:40,641
When LotHold finds a gap or a concern, it

39
00:01:40,661 --> 00:01:43,074
doesn't simply give the shipment a pass or

40
00:01:43,094 --> 00:01:46,306
fail. It shows the issue and links it back to

41
00:01:46,326 --> 00:01:47,709
the supporting information.

42
00:01:48,028 --> 00:01:50,468
The reviewer can then generate a request

43
00:01:50,488 --> 00:01:52,862
asking the supplier for the specific evidence

44
00:01:52,882 --> 00:01:55,494
needed. Instead of a vague request for more

45
00:01:55,514 --> 00:01:58,034
documents, the supplier gets a clearer

46
00:01:58,054 --> 00:02:00,080
explanation of what's missing.

47
00:02:01,456 --> 00:02:03,603
Once the evidence has been examined, the

48
00:02:03,623 --> 00:02:05,784
reviewer records what they checked, their

49
00:02:05,804 --> 00:02:08,557
reasoning, and any supporting documents.

50
00:02:08,876 --> 00:02:11,316
LotHold keeps that decision connected to the

51
00:02:11,336 --> 00:02:13,105
evidence used during the review.

52
00:02:13,424 --> 00:02:15,804
If important information changes afterward,

53
00:02:15,963 --> 00:02:18,469
the previous decision may need to be reviewed

54
00:02:18,490 --> 00:02:21,436
again. This is about making the process easier

55
00:02:21,456 --> 00:02:24,228
to inspect, not automatically certifying the

56
00:02:24,248 --> 00:02:25,117
shipment.

57
00:02:26,474 --> 00:02:29,274
LotHold also supports preparing structured

58
00:02:29,294 --> 00:02:30,783
due-diligence records.

59
00:02:31,102 --> 00:02:33,316
A due-diligence statement is a formal

60
00:02:33,336 --> 00:02:35,909
declaration supported by the information a

61
00:02:35,929 --> 00:02:37,299
business has reviewed.

62
00:02:37,618 --> 00:02:40,537
Any generated XML is a draft for further

63
00:02:40,557 --> 00:02:42,545
validation and submission through the

64
00:02:42,565 --> 00:02:44,493
appropriate official process.

65
00:02:44,812 --> 00:02:47,542
It is not proof of regulatory acceptance.

66
00:02:48,904 --> 00:02:51,477
What you've seen is a real LotHold engineering

67
00:02:51,497 --> 00:02:54,402
test workflow, using clearly labelled test

68
00:02:54,422 --> 00:02:56,976
records rather than an actual cocoa shipment.

69
00:02:57,308 --> 00:03:00,074
The hosted pilot has demonstrated uploads,

70
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:03,365
satellite analysis, PDF generation, and

71
00:03:03,385 --> 00:03:04,436
evidence recovery.

72
00:03:04,755 --> 00:03:07,926
Our goal is simple: make cocoa supply-chain

73
00:03:07,946 --> 00:03:11,197
evidence easier to organize, investigate, and

74
00:03:11,217 --> 00:03:13,389
review. This is LotHold.
